WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.956
Most LLM inference can be understood as two related phases: prefill and decode.

2
00:00:04.956 --> 00:00:08.876
Prefill is the work of reading the prompt and existing context.

3
00:00:08.876 --> 00:00:18.195
If the user sends a long document, a repository excerpt, or a large instruction block, prefill can dominate the time before the model starts speaking.

4
00:00:18.195 --> 00:00:23.225
Decode is the phase that produces new tokens after the prompt has been processed.

5
00:00:23.225 --> 00:00:26.553
Batching often helps these phases in different ways.

6
00:00:26.553 --> 00:00:35.503
During prefill, the runtime may be able to process larger blocks of prompt tokens efficiently because there is a lot of parallel work available.

7
00:00:35.503 --> 00:00:44.009
During decode, each active sequence usually needs the next token before it can move forward, so the shape of the work is more incremental.

8
00:00:44.009 --> 00:00:49.556
That is why prompt processing speed and generation speed should be measured separately.

9
00:00:49.556 --> 00:00:55.917
First-token latency is the time between submitting the request and seeing the first generated token.

10
00:00:55.917 --> 00:01:02.944
It is strongly affected by prompt length, queueing, prefill efficiency, and how many other requests are active.

11
00:01:02.944 --> 00:01:07.973
Tokens per second measures generation speed once the model is producing output.

12
00:01:07.973 --> 00:01:13.669
Requests per second matters more for services that handle many users or automated clients.

13
00:01:13.669 --> 00:01:21.435
A workload made of long prompts and short answers behaves differently from a workload made of short prompts and long answers.

14
00:01:21.435 --> 00:01:24.467
Long prompts stress prefill and context memory.

15
00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:28.018
Long responses stress decode and sustained generation.

16
00:01:28.018 --> 00:01:38.077
Good tuning begins by naming the workload clearly, because a batch configuration is only good relative to the kind of requests the system actually needs to serve.

17
00:01:38.077 --> 00:01:44.290
When reviewing benchmark output, avoid collapsing prefill and decode into one vague speed number.

18
00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:51.538
A configuration can be excellent at absorbing a long prompt but ordinary during generation, or the other way around.

19
00:01:51.538 --> 00:02:01.154
Separate measurements help you decide whether to tune for faster first response, smoother streaming, or better total capacity under repeated requests.
